From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 05:24:19 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08041/11362] Two repository defects root-caused: worktree runs
 write the canonical repo, and the ARC failure count is a random variable

Both were surfaced by the adversarial review as observations; this commit records the
mechanisms, measured rather than argued.

1. A MUTATION HARNESS RUN FROM A WORKTREE REWRITES THE CANONICAL REPO. Every harness
   does `REPO = "/home/ianblenke/github.com/ianblenke/carnot"` then
   `subprocess.run([... pytest ...], cwd=REPO)`, and experiment scripts write artifacts
   to CWD-relative paths (`RESULT_PATH = Path("results/experiment_1035_....json")`), so
   the write lands on the canonical artifact whatever tree launched it. Confirmed in six
   harnesses. Plain `pytest` from a worktree is NOT affected -- verified directly: a full
   `-k arc` run in a worktree left canonical `git status` unchanged. Three candidate
   fixes recorded; none applied, since (a) derive REPO from the harness's own
   `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` is the operator's call.

2. THE ARC FAILURE COUNT IS ORDER-UNSTABLE. Same command, same clean tree, same
   `-p no:randomly`: 31 failed then 25 failed. Three tests appear in the failure set on
   some samples and not others and all three PASS in isolation; `4676`'s failure is
   `KeyError: 'enable_subgoal_search'`, cross-test state leaking between xdist workers.
   Baseline at 30f9fea80 is 52 then 53. So this tree is 25-31 failed / ~8242 passed
   against a 52-53 baseline: 30 previously-failing ARC tests now pass and ZERO confirmed
   new failures -- but "all N reproduce at HEAD" is not a well-formed claim while the
   set is a random variable, and a new failure must be disconfirmed in isolation before
   it is dismissed, as these three were.

Also corrects the known-issues entry that carried Phase 2's "1 of 5 to 3 of 5" as though
it were a funnel number; it is parse-level and offline, the live funnel is unchanged at
1 of 6, and the net new STRONG game is sc25 alone at n=1, p=1.000.

